#clinical-finding(title: [POTS Heart-Rate Threshold Does Not Reliably Predict Symptom Burden; a Non-POTS Dysautonomia Population May Be Under-Recognized])[

The diagnostic HR-rise threshold for postural orthostatic tachycardia syndrome (POTS) — $gt.eq 30$ bpm in adults, $gt.eq 40$ bpm in adolescents within 10 minutes of standing — does not reliably predict symptomatic burden, and a dysautonomic population may fall below it yet remain under-recognized, untreated, and excluded from research.

- *International multidisciplinary expert consensus (Sivakoti et al. 2026):* A modified-Delphi panel of 40 contributors from 7 countries (adult and pediatric POTS/dysautonomia specialists plus patient-advocacy representatives) reached very strong (>90%) to unanimous (100%) consensus on all 31 statements, with 24 unanimous. The consensus explicitly critiques the HR-rise threshold as too restrictive and calls for recognition of a "non-POTS dysautonomia" population — patients with orthostatic intolerance, autonomic dysfunction, and functional impairment who do not meet the HR rise. It identifies standardized nomenclature and classification as an unmet need @Sivakoti2026POTSConsensus. Note: consensus unanimity reflects agreement among invited experts, not validation against outcome data; patient-advocacy membership is a legitimacy signal, not an epistemic weight.

- *Parsaik et al. 2013 (Mayo Clinic, n=184: 84 POTS + 100 orthostatic intolerance without tachycardia):* Patients with orthostatic intolerance but without postural tachycardia share the clinical presentation, autonomic findings, and deconditioning of POTS; POTS patients showed only mildly greater dysautonomia. This provides partial empirical basis for the non-POTS dysautonomia construct, though these patients still met orthostatic-intolerance criteria — they were not a threshold-free population @Parsaik2013OInoTachycardia.

- *Boris et al. 2020 (CHOP, pediatric chronic orthostatic intolerance):* Across 28 symptoms, only insomnia differed between an HR rise of 30–39 bpm versus $gt.eq 40$ bpm. This compares two bands both above the $gt.eq 30$ threshold, so it supports adjusting the cutoff (the pediatric $gt.eq 40$ value may be too high) rather than abolishing the threshold or establishing a population below $gt.eq 30$; it is not a direct test of "non-POTS dysautonomia" @Boris2020HRnotPredictive.

- *Chung and Raj 2026 (JAMA review):* Documents the current consensus criteria, ~70% functional impairment, a 24-month diagnostic delay, and 30–40% post-infectious onset — the context of under-recognition the consensus responds to, though the review itself affirms the $gt.eq 30$ criterion as current consensus rather than independently establishing under-recognition @ChungRaj2026POTSReview.

*Important distinction:* Orthostatic intolerance (a broad symptom domain) is not the same as the POTS heart-rate phenotype (a narrow criterion). In ME/CFS, only a minority of patients with orthostatic symptoms meet formal POTS criteria; most orthostatic symptoms fall outside the threshold by design. The consensus's recognition claim concerns a *dysautonomic* population with functional impairment — not every orthostatic-symptom patient, who may have a different (non-POTS) diagnosis or deconditioning. The $gt.eq 30$ criterion was never intended to capture all orthostatic intolerance, only the tachycardia phenotype.

*Relevance to ME/CFS:* This is a POTS/dysautonomia-scoped finding (not an ME/CFS-cohort study). It matters for ME/CFS because orthostatic intolerance is a core IOM symptom domain and 70–90% of ME/CFS patients show orthostatic intolerance on testing; a threshold that misses subthreshold dysautonomia risks under-recognizing an important comorbidity and excluding patients from trials. Severity applicability: unknown — the source papers do not stratify by severity.

*Interpretive caution:* Whether subthreshold dysautonomia is the same autonomic deficit as POTS along a continuous spectrum, or a distinct entity, is not resolved by the available cross-sectional data — Parsaik 2013 shows shared presentation and findings but not a shared trajectory, and no study yet demonstrates that reclassifying subthreshold patients changes outcomes. The recognition claim should not be read as a proven mechanistic identity.

*Certainty:* 0.44 discounted (raw 0.55, consensus/registry evidence, POTS population weight 0.80). Expert consensus plus supportive single-center registry data; no ME/CFS-cohort replication.

*Consequence:* Clinicians assessing orthostatic symptoms should not rely on the HR-rise threshold alone to decide whether dysautonomia is present — a patient with orthostatic intolerance and functional impairment below the HR threshold may still have treatable autonomic dysfunction, and should not be excluded from research or management.

